Consider a classical (or quantum) many-body system whose microscopic dynamics are governed by time-reversible equations of motion (Hamilton's equations, or unitary Schrödinger evolution). Let \(\Gamma\) denote the phase space (or Hilbert space) of the system, and let \(T\) be the time-reversal involution. The microscopic dynamics satisfy: if \(t \mapsto x(t)\) is a solution then so is \(t \mapsto T x(-t)\). Macroscopic observables are defined as coarse-grained functionals \(M_\alpha:\Gamma\to\mathbb{R}\) obtained by partitioning \(\Gamma\) into macrostates, and a statistical description is given by a time-dependent probability distribution \(\rho_t\) on \(\Gamma\), with thermodynamic entropy identified (up to choices) with \(S[\rho_t] = -k_B \int \rho_t \log \rho_t\) or with a Boltzmann-type coarse-grained entropy \(S_B(M) = k_B \log |\Gamma_M|\).

Formulate and justify a general framework — specifying (i) the class of admissible initial conditions (or a measure on them), (ii) the coarse-graining procedure that defines macrostates, and (iii) the mechanism by which microscopic correlations among the dynamical variables decay or are truncated — under which one can rigorously derive, from the reversible microscopic dynamics alone together with these three ingredients, a macroscopic evolution equation for \(\{M_\alpha(t)\}\) and an associated entropy functional \(S(t)\) such that:

(a) \(\frac{dS}{dt} \ge 0\) for all admissible initial data, with equality only at equilibrium;
(b) the macroscopic evolution exhibits a definite arrow of time (Eddington's time-arrow) despite the time-reversal symmetry of the underlying dynamics;
(c) the framework is internally consistent: the assumptions on initial conditions, coarse graining, and correlation damping do not implicitly smuggle in irreversibility, and they reduce to the known phenomenological irreversible thermodynamics (Boltzmann, Onsager, hydrodynamic limits) in appropriate regimes.

Equivalently: identify the minimal additional postulates beyond Hamiltonian (or unitary) microscopic mechanics that are necessary and sufficient to produce the second law of thermodynamics as a theorem about the coarse-grained macroscopic evolution.

